{"id": "raraglow", "kind": "influencer · di luar niche", "account": "@rara.glow, influencer skincare", "subject": "the influencer's dismissal of the karhutla haze as normal", "recorded": "2026-10-30", "episodes": [{"text": "Guys, asap di Kalimantan itu tiap tahun ada, jangan lebay dong. Yang penting kulit tetap aman: double cleanse, SPF 50, dan serum antioksidan. Link produk di bio, diskon sampai Minggu.", "result": {"text": "Guys, asap di Kalimantan itu tiap tahun ada, jangan lebay dong. Yang penting kulit tetap aman: double cleanse, SPF 50, dan serum antioksidan. Link produk di bio, diskon sampai Minggu.", "context": "Forest and peat fires (karhutla) have burned since July 2026 (about 95,000 hectares in July alone); Kalimantan and Sumatra have breathed toxic haze for four months, worst on 13-15 October, with schools closed and ojol and street vendors losing income. Malaysia and Singapore protested the cross-border haze, a class action is being prepared, and NGOs, clerics and regional leaders demand national-disaster status, which the government has not granted. President Prabowo said he hoped for rain, which netizens mocked ('then what is the government for?'); a governor went viral having a lavish breakfast abroad during the haze, and influencers complained Instagram throttled their karhutla posts. The dominant narrative: the government lets corporate land burn and understates the crisis. Netizens have just punished influencers seen as tone-deaf: Vilmei's 'Day 1 Cari Kerja' job-hunt parody and Irenna Kristyn's mockery of cost-of-living protests both drew mass criticism and forced apologies in October.", "tokens": 347866, "segments": ["s_beauty", "s_fashion", "s_parent_muda"], "anchors": [{"id": "b3_mixue", "similarity": 0.9, "mix": {"negative": 0.0325, "neutral": 0.82, "positive": 0.1475}, "n": 400}, {"id": "b1_esteh_somasi", "similarity": 0.66, "mix": {"negative": 0.8169014084507042, "neutral": 0.10641627543035993, "positive": 0.07668231611893583}, "n": 639}, {"